  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nDiffluent southwest flow within the RE region of the subtropical\njet lead to isolated supercells and severe weather this evening.\n00Z RAOB at KEPZ confirmed favorable wind shear and an increase in\nmoisture. Precipitable water is now 1.0-1.1\" with 30-40 knots of\nEBWS and a 45 knot speed max at 400mb, enough for some very strong\nstorms across SW NM today.\n\nPrevailing weather pattern depicts a diving Pacific low moving\nsouth down the California coast. This low will play a major role\nin late-week rain chances for the El Paso area. To the south, high\npressure aloft is building over S TX and NE Mexico. SSW flow aloft\nwill continue to promote moisture advection out of the south,\nleading to an extended period of rain chances for late-October.\n\nFor Tuesday temperatures will continue to warm a bit, reaching \nthe lower 80s across most of the desert lowlands. Isolated showers\nand thunderstorms are expected, again favoring SW NM and the high\nterrain. Coverage will be lower, generally 10-20% given warmer \nair aloft eating away at instability. Still, favorable 0-6km wind\nshear profiles could result in a couple strong to severe storms\nacross Grant/Hidalgo Counties. Very similar conditions on \nWednesday, with scattered showers and thunderstorms favoring SW \nNM during the afternoon and evening hours.\n\nForecast remains on track for widespread rain chances\nThursday/Friday across Southern New Mexico and Far West Texas.\nPacific low will move ashore late this week, with a 75-knot jet\nstreak rounding the base of the system over the Southwest US.\nDeep moisture flux out of Mexico will push precipitable water\nvalues to 1.2-1.4\", well above normal for this time of year.\nWidespread showers and thunderstorms developing across E AZ and W\nNM will slowly move east across the forecast area. Storms will be\ncapable of heavy rain and flash flooding. Current guidance put the\nhighest flood risk over W NM, including Grant County and the Gila\nWilderness (most likely rain amounts 1.00-1.50\", worse-case \n2.00-3.00\" localized). \n\nModels continue to struggle with the evolution of this system,\nleaving remnant energy behind and continuing rain chances into \nthe upcoming weekend. This comes at fairly low confidence right \nnow, given the weaker forcing. This, however, is something the\narea should get used to given a winter outlook that suggests above\nnormal precipitation and more frequent Pacific storm systems.",
